WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.360
How can a cell answer a signal in about a millisecond?

2
00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:11.040
Most receptors hand their message to something else, such as a G protein or a

3
00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:13.860
kinase. An ion channel hands it to nothing.

4
00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:19.040
The signal opens a hole, ions pour through, and the voltage across the membrane

5
00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:24.600
changes. One open channel lets about ten million ions cross every second, so which

6
00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:25.600
way do they go?

7
00:00:27.333 --> 00:00:30.113
Two kinds of protein move ions across a membrane.

8
00:00:30.853 --> 00:00:35.553
A pump binds its ions and changes shape to carry them, uphill if it spends ATP.

9
00:00:35.553 --> 00:00:40.353
A channel is a pore with a gate, which a transmitter, a voltage, or heat can open.

10
00:00:41.113 --> 00:00:45.653
Once it is open, ions run downhill at around ten million a second, while a pump

11
00:00:45.653 --> 00:00:46.833
manages a few hundred.

12
00:00:47.473 --> 00:00:50.733
So pumps build the gradients slowly, and channels spend them fast.

13
00:00:53.217 --> 00:00:58.237
Downhill has two parts: a push from the difference in concentration, and a pull from

14
00:00:58.237 --> 00:00:59.617
the voltage across the membrane.

15
00:01:00.317 --> 00:01:05.297
Take sodium. A cell keeps it mostly outside, and the inside of a resting neuron sits

16
00:01:05.297 --> 00:01:06.937
near minus −70 mV.

17
00:01:07.537 --> 00:01:10.957
For each ion, there is one voltage at which push and pull balance.

18
00:01:11.477 --> 00:01:16.637
That is its equilibrium potential, E, and for sodium it is about plus +60 mV.

19
00:01:18.967 --> 00:01:21.587
A cell keeps potassium mostly inside.

20
00:01:22.227 --> 00:01:26.927
With typical concentrations, E for potassium is about minus −90 mV.

21
00:01:27.407 --> 00:01:31.307
An open channel drags the voltage toward the E of the one ion it passes.

22
00:01:31.807 --> 00:01:34.887
So the direction of flow follows from one subtraction.

23
00:01:35.527 --> 00:01:38.627
The driving force is the membrane voltage minus E.

24
00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:46.760
At minus −70 mV, sodium is 130 mV away from its E. So it rushes in, and the cell

25
00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:50.140
depolarises, meaning its voltage becomes less negative.

26
00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:56.260
Potassium is only 20 mV from its E. So it leaks out gently, and pulls the voltage

27
00:01:56.260 --> 00:01:57.260
back down.

28
00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:02.530
Very few ions need to cross to change the voltage.

29
00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:06.410
So the concentrations barely shift, and nothing has to be made.

30
00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:08.370
The voltage is the message.

31
00:02:08.930 --> 00:02:13.210
So keep this rule: the signal opens a hole, and the gradient does the rest.
